it more focused so I came up  with a half-hour Hegel series a line by  line paragraph by paragraph commentary  skipping nothing in the entire text and  I thought I'd be finished with it in  just a few years I'm still working on it  at the present with well over 200 videos  in the series but there is light at the  end of the tunnel we are now in the  600's in the 800 or so paragraph work I  came up with a new format for my videos  in this half-hour Hegel series and you  can see a little bit of it here I'm  gonna jump through it kind of quickly  first I start with this master slide  with me and Hegel side-by-side  that's a younger me and then it's this  this particular first video has been  explaining what I'm doing in the project  itself but most of them will then begin  with a slide that shows the paragraph  and then its side by side me reading  Hegel's text and then we cut again to  another slide and then we've got me in  front of the track board explaining what  that that particular paragraph of Hegel  that I've just read to the listener is  about at least in my view Hegel is a  very difficult thinker so of course many  people might have disagreements with  what I'm saying but this is the first  thing like it out there for not just  this work but really any other work  doing a very close reading line by line  by line and explaining it for the  watcher or listener  in 2014 I started partnering for the  first time with local libraries not to  give one-off talks as I'd done in the  past but to do entire series so my first  one was this glimpses into existence  eleven key existentialist writers which  I did at the Kingston library in New  York and we went through 11 different  figures that are really central to the  existentialist movement and it went  quite well it ended up gathering sort of  a critical mass of regular attendees  I was invited back and since I was doing  a lot of work on philosophical  conceptions of anger and anger  management at the time I thought it  would be cool to do a set of lectures  specifically on that you can see I began  with Greek tragedy and epic poetry and  then went into Jewish Scriptures but  then went into Plato and Aristotle and  we got as far as Thomas Aquinas before I  ended up with my wife leaving Kingston  to come here to Milwaukee otherwise we  had planned to have an entire 12 lecture  series that year  since coming back to our home state of  Wisconsin and our hometown of Milwaukee  I started doing more of these public  lectures one of the places I was invited  as the Frank Weinberg where I since 2016  have been doing a quarterly series  called philosophers in the midst of  history in which I talk about  philosophers who they're engaged with  their political background economic  background historical background and the  mark that they themselves left on  history and that's going on for another  year here and probably will be for years  to come  an even bigger project has been a  monthly series which I've missed a few  due to illnesses called worlds of  speculative fiction so for the last four  years every month I've been coming in  and we had people reading certain texts  and we talked about the narrative world  created by important fantasy science  fiction horror alternate history all  sorts of things utopian literature all  these interesting thinkers and I loved  going back and rereading many of these  writers and and again that one's  continuing on into the present as well  and I don't think we're ever going to  run out of authors to discuss  I started getting people sending me  books wanting me to review them so I  started this whole new series called  Sadler's honest book reviews I'm seeing  a little excerpt of one right now the  porch and the cross and I would you know  shoot in my studio and discuss the books  and do so in a very unvarnished way  saying what I thought was good and what  was bad and whether the book was worth  actually having for the most part I've  given pretty positive reviews I have  said recently that one of the books  wasn't worth kidding and I have been  quite critical on certain points of the  books  that's another series that I'm probably  going to continue on for the indefinite  future because I've got quite a stack of  books waiting to be reviewed  I've also been doing some live-streaming  here you see me in one of my AMAs that I  was holding fairly recently I think yeah  that one looks like it was in the  hallway no not in the hallway in the  bedroom you can see the closet behind  and what I do is I answer questions that  are put to me by my viewers in the chat  function usually those go for about an  hour and a half there are a lot of fun  and I also do pop-ups when I have the  time  all of those are under written by my  patreon supporters when I reached a  certain level of patreon support I  pledged that I would start doing these  AMA and pop-up sessions and I've been  pretty good about getting them in each  month here there you see me like showing  the bookshelf off so you get the idea of  what those are like  in 2018 I started another series called  ideas that matter in which I would have  sit-down conversations with people who I  thought had something really interesting  to say and contribute to very often  these are people who I I know and I have  you could say some chemistry with and we  get into some great discussions often  veering off topic and coming back on you  see Sheila Payton here one of our great  Milwaukee local authors and and she's  involved in the community quite a bit I  got to know her through the writing  group that were both involved in and  then here's another one with Chris Gill  that I shot when I was in London for  stoical on 2018 and we had a great  conversation about his forthcoming book  about stoicism and virtue ethics and I'm  going to start doing more of these as  the new millennium the new decade  approaches and I'm really looking  forward to having more of these  conversations and chats with all these  very interesting people  another series that I actually started  this year is advice for lifelong  learners and people engaged in  self-directed study about how to  approach particular thinkers or  movements in philosophy so far I've  mostly done just things in ancient and a  little bit of medieval but I intend to  roll these out and expand it to include  all sorts of thinkers across the wide  spectrum of approaches and throughout  the timeline of history and hopefully  these will be useful for people I think  I'll be rolling out some adjunct videos  as well about how to successfully  approach philosophical texts I did one  like that years and years ago for my  students which really needs to be  updated but that's one of the main  series that I'm working on now I try to  roll out one of those each month  so there that's quite a lot of YouTube  content over the course of this decade  and I have no plans to quit anytime soon  I'm hoping that YouTube is gonna stay  around its present format who knows what  technology is gonna look like ten years  from now think about the radical  transformations that we've had just in  the course of this decade and how many  new features have been rolled out so who  knows what's gonna happen I can tell you  this I'm gonna keep on doing the talk  series and uploading core concept videos  and half hour hagel videos and doing  interviews and book reviews and AMAs and  all these sorts of things and I'll  probably have some other things lined up  as well as new ideas occur to me so you  know if any of this stuff interests you  can always go back and find most of this  I think in playlist devoted specifically  to it if you've missed out on some of  the things that you might want to check  up on and you know if you've got ideas  for what you'd like to see most of the  time I'm not looking for advice from  people but if you want to leave them as  comments on this and you know dream big  I'll do my my unusual thing which is to  say yeah that sounds pretty good can't  say that I'll actually take on all those  ideas because probably I won't have you  know the available time even if you know  patreon radically boosts up and I all I  can do is spend time doing YouTube  videos the amount of stuff that people  ask for from you can't possibly be met  by one content producer but I can get  quite a bit done I will say a few other  things before I close this I'm fortunate  in that I've got a base of viewers and  subscribers who really do like the  content that I am producing and share it  with other people and and help support  the the channel and the work that I'm  doing many of the comments that  it are quite encouraging many of the  comments that I get like I put are also  quite heartening in that people are  saying this is really helpful for me for  the classes I'm taking for my own  lifelong learning in order to help me  get through this paper this this test  with this professor who won't explain  anything so that's all quite gratifying  I really enjoy the interactions that I  have with with viewers and subscribers  through the AMAs and in discussions of  content I've gotten to be friends with  quite a few of them over these last 10  years or so of YouTube content so a lot  of good things coming up you know  hopefully remain healthy and keep  churning out the content on my  chalkboard back here so you know what  we'll see what happens in this next  decade I hope it's a healthy and  prosperous one for you on your end and  that you have a great new year  you
 

